# Test: ddos direction classification -- an attack on a box-owned (local) victim is
# classified Direction=local, surfaced on the incident, AND enforced on the box.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-ddos-direction-allowlist AC-13/AC-9 (classification) -- an attack on
#            a box-owned address (127.0.0.3, RTN_LOCAL) opens an incident whose
#            direction field is "local" (show ddos incidents), proving the detector
#            classifies direction via iface.AddressIsLocal end-to-end.
# VALIDATES: spec-fixit-firewall-concurrency-deadlock AC-7 -- the classification
#            reaches the dataplane: ddos-local installs an on-host drop covering
#            127.0.0.3/32, reported by `show ddos local`. The narrow
#            classification-only scope this test used to carry is RETIRED.
# PREVENTS:  direction being unset/wrong (e.g. defaulting to remote) for a box-owned
#            victim, and a classified attack that nothing enforces.
#
# WHY THE SCOPE WAS NARROW, and why it is not any more. Loading the firewall nft
# backend alongside the ddos-local responder under a flood used to wedge the
# daemon's command dispatch, so this test dropped both the firewall block and the
# drop assertion. Every mechanism that could produce that hang is now fixed at
# the source:
#   - reconcileMu serializes the whole ApplyAll body, so at most one owner is
#     ever inside Backend.Apply (internal/component/firewall/registry.go).
#   - both backends bound the netlink round-trip at 10s, so a wedged dataplane
#     returns an error instead of blocking forever
#     (internal/plugins/firewall/nft/deadline_linux.go,
#     internal/plugins/firewall/vpp/timeout_linux.go).
#   - status() reads the published snapshot through an atomic.Pointer and takes
#     no lock, so show ddos local never waits on a reconcile
#     (internal/plugins/ddos/local/responder.go).
# The drop assertion is restored here. The other half of the workaround, a
# `firewall {}` block present WHILE the responder mitigates, is proven by
# test/plugin/ddos-firewall-concurrency.ci rather than duplicated here: both
# tests share the ddos-flood exclusive group, so a second flood of the same
# length would cost the QEMU suite that time and assert nothing new. This test
# reaches the backend through the ApplyAll autoload path instead
# (internal/component/firewall/registry.go), as ddos-transit-forward-drop.ci does.
#
# STATUS: option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf. Full daemon path on the QEMU Alpine VM via an in-daemon
#   observer plugin (ze_api handshake), same harness as ddos-bps-amplification.

# This test needs a fully privileged host, not merely Linux, and needs TWO
# distinct capability families -- which is why it declares both.
#   - bpf (CAP_BPF + CAP_SYS_RESOURCE): traffic-usage calls
#     rlimit.RemoveMemlock() and then link.AttachTCX()
#     (internal/plugins/trafficusage/attach_linux.go:33,46). On the unprivileged
#     GitHub runner the rlimit call failed with "need CAP_BPF/CAP_SYS_RESOURCE",
#     eBPF accounting ran inert, and no incident was ever opened.
#   - net-admin: AttachTCX programs the interface's tc hooks, a privileged
#     network operation.
# Declaring only one of the two would fail OPEN: a host holding just that one
# passes the gate and the test fails exactly as it did with no marker at all
# (ai/rules/fail-closed-guards.md, ai/rules/qemu-testing.md).
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf
# Every ddos test floods the SAME loopback interface, and the detector picks the
# victim by top-destination-bytes over that interface's counters, so a sibling's
# concurrent flood is indistinguishable from this test's own. Unique victim
# addresses only stop the bind collision; they cannot partition the counters.
# Without this, QEMU pass 4 had test 155 resolve 127.0.0.4 (test 157's victim)
# and this test resolve no victim at all (direction fell back to remote).
option=exclusive:group=ddos-flood
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.detect:value=info
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.local:value=info
# 45s for the classification poll plus 30s for the drop poll, and the two are
# sequential worst cases rather than alternatives. 90s left no room for the
# second budget: a slow classification would have hit the runner's timeout, which
# reports "the daemon never finished" instead of "no drop was installed".
option=timeout:value=130s

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=130s
option=asn:value=65001
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_PEER


stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
ddos {
	detect {
		enabled true
		absolute-floor 1000
		confirm-duration 1
		startup-grace 0
		baseline-window 10
		check-interval 1
		characterize-enable false
	}
	observe {
		incident-ring-size 100
	}
	local {
		response-level enforce
	}
}

traffic {
	usage {
		enabled true
		track-ip true
		interfaces {
			interface lo {
				enabled true
			}
		}
	}
}

plugin {
	external ddos-direction-probe {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/ddos-direction"
		encoder json
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast {
					prefix {
						maximum 10000
					}
				}
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=125s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=LOCAL-DROP-INSTALLED 127.0.0.3
expect=stderr:contains=ddos-local: drop rule installed
expect=stderr:contains=hook=ingress
